// bridge — the shape every other language starts with, met last.
//
//   lux run bridge.lux
//
// Every other program in this directory runs top to bottom, because that is what lux
// does and what a first year needs. This one has a `main`. It is the only one, and
// that is the point of it.
//
// A `main` buys a beginner nothing. lux starts at the first line, so an entry point
// is ceremony with no payoff — which is why it is the last thing lux teaches instead
// of the first, and why nothing else here uses one. But Rust and Go both require a
// named entry point, and that requirement is the last difference left between a lux
// program and a real one in either language.
//
// So this program exists to be converted. Run it:
//
//   lux convert rust bridge.lux
//   lux convert go   bridge.lux
//
// and read what comes back. It is very nearly this file: the same four functions in
// the same order, `main` mapped straight onto the target's own, and none of the
// machinery that makes richer values print alike, because everything here is
// arithmetic and strings.
//
// Very nearly, and the exception is the interesting part. One function you did not
// write comes along — a division guard, four lines of it — because `celsius * 9 / 5`
// can divide by zero in principle and lux promised you a sentence about it rather than
// a crash. That is the honest shape of graduation. You do not leave with nothing added;
// you leave with a short, readable list of the things the language was doing for you,
// and now you can see all of them.
//
// Swift is the cousin already across. Its top level *is* the entry point, exactly like
// lux's, so a `main` here becomes an ordinary Swift function and a call to it. That is
// the honest translation rather than a special case, and it is the reason the bridge
// is Rust's and Go's and not Swift's.

// Whole-number arithmetic on purpose: 9 / 5 truncates, so this is the textbook's
// integer conversion and not the exact one. Every language on the other side does the
// same thing with the same operators, which is part of what makes it a fair bridge.
func fahrenheit(celsius: int) -> int {
    return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32
}

// Right-aligning a number by growing a string until it is wide enough. No array
// indexing anywhere in this program, which is deliberate: an index would pull in the
// bounds check lux performs for you, and the translation would stop being only your
// program.
func pad(n: int, width: int) -> string {
    var s = string(n)
    while length(s) < width {
        s = " " + s
    }
    return s
}

func line(celsius: int) -> string {
    return pad(celsius, 6) + pad(fahrenheit(celsius), 8)
}

func main() {
    print("celsius   fahrenheit")
    for step in 0..8 {
        print(line(-40 + step * 20))
    }
    print("")
    print("water freezes at", fahrenheit(0))
    print("water boils at  ", fahrenheit(100))
    print("they agree at   ", fahrenheit(-40))
}
